 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Regulation",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The part is an inductive buck converter running in continuous mode. It senses LED current on the high side across RS and switches its internal LX transistor to hold the mean sense voltage at 100 mV, with ±15 % hysteresis; the switching frequency can reach 1 MHz."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Setting the current",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "RS sets the nominal LED current as 0.1/RS with ADJ open. RS should be a 1 % part with adequate power rating and a low temperature coefficient so the current stays stable."
  },
  {
   "topic": "DC dimming",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "A DC voltage on ADJ between 0.3 V and 1.2 V scales the current linearly, from 25 % to 100 % of IOUT_NOM. Any level from 1.2 V to 5 V clamps the current at the 1.2 V value. ADJ sees about 500 kΩ to the internal 1.2 V reference."
  },
  {
   "topic": "PWM dimming",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "A PWM signal on ADJ gates the output; the average current is the RS setting times the duty cycle. The signal swings 0 V to 5 V, with high above 1.2 V and low below 0.2 V, and must drive the 500 kΩ internal pull-up. The dimming ratio reaches 1200:1. An open-drain microcontroller output can drive ADJ through a resistor with a Schottky clamp to ground, which stops negative spikes that would upset the output current."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Shutdown",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Pulling ADJ below 0.2 V stops switching and drops supply current to about 120 µA. The part turns back on when ADJ rises past VADJ_ON, 0.25 V typical."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Open and short LED protection",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "With the LED string open, the inductor is isolated from LX and the part is not damaged. Protection against open-circuit and short-circuit LEDs is inherent to the part."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal shutdown",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Switching stops at a junction temperature of 150 °C typical and resumes about 20 °C lower."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input capacitor",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Use a low-ESR input capacitor: it supplies the inductor's peak current and its ESR adds to the source impedance and costs efficiency. 100 µF is recommended from a DC supply; above 220 µF from a rectified 12 V AC transformer, whose ±10 % ripple otherwise dips below the string voltage and lowers the LED current. The 0.1 µF bypass C2 at VIN and GND is always required."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Inductor",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "47 µH to 220 µH is the recommended range. Larger values suit higher supply voltages and lower currents: they cut the errors from switching delays and flatten current against supply. The inductor's saturation current must exceed the peak current and its rated current the mean: above 1.2 A saturation for 700 mA output, above 500 mA for 350 mA output. Pick L so that duty cycle and on and off times stay inside their limits over the supply and load range; a duty near 50 % at the nominal supply gives the best balance of overshoot and undershoot and the best temperature stability."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Diode",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "D1 should be a fast Schottky with low capacitance and low reverse leakage at the highest voltage and temperature; leakage when hot adds dissipation. Its peak rating must exceed the inductor peak current and its continuous rating the load current. A silicon diode's higher forward drop and recovery overshoot raise the LX peak, which with supply ripple must stay under the LX maximum."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output ripple",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "A 1 µF capacitor across the LED string reduces ripple current roughly threefold, and larger values reduce it further. It leaves frequency and efficiency unchanged but slows the rise of LED voltage and so delays start-up."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Low supply voltage",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "An internal regulator holds off the switch drive until the supply passes a start-up threshold at which the switch resistance is low enough. Below the 6 V minimum the duty cycle and dissipation run high, which should be avoided. With two or more LEDs the string voltage normally keeps the part from switching below about 6 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "The junction should stay under 125 °C for full performance. Dissipation is limited by θJA = 132.6 °C/W: 0.94 W at 25 °C, falling linearly to zero at 125 °C on the derating curve. Poor efficiency from an unsuitable inductor or excess capacitance on LX adds to it."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Put the 0.1 µF bypass right at VIN and GND to stop ground bounce from the distant supply return. LX is the fast switching node: keep its traces short. Solder the GND pin straight to the ground plane. Mount the inductor and input capacitor close to the part with low-resistance connections, and allow for copper resistance that adds to RS. ADJ is high impedance: keep its trace short when it is open, route high-voltage traces away from it to avoid leakage that would shift the current, and ring it with ground."
  }
 ],
